  "desks": [
    {
      "title": "Claims intake — why 31% of claims need rework",
      "description": "Current-state analysis for a claims modernisation: 12 weeks of intake data, the process as it really runs, and a hand-coded set of 200 reworked claims.",
      "dueInDays": 5,
      "brief": "## Claims intake — current-state analysis\n\n**Initiative** Claims intake modernisation · Kestrel Mutual, home and contents\n**Sponsor** Helena, GM Claims · **Steering group** @+5d\n**Evidence** 12 weeks of lodged claims from the claims system, 200 reworked claims coded by hand, and interviews with the triage team\n\n### What the data says\n- **25,560 claims lodged, 7,931 reworked — 31%.** Rework means triage sent the claim back for more information before assessment could start\n- The storm in week 7 lifted volume by three-quarters, and rework rose to 34% with it\n- Days to first contact average 2.8, and go above 4 in the storm weeks\n- Three causes account for two-thirds of rework: missing photos, policy number mismatch and the wrong claim type\n\n### What it means\nRework is a lodgement problem, not an assessment one. Most of it could be caught on the form, before a claim ever reaches triage. If the form caught all three top causes, rework would fall to about 10.5% at best — which is why release 1 aims for under 15%, not zero.\n\n### Still to confirm\nWhether phone lodgements rework more than online ones. The extract has channel totals by week but not channel per claim — IT is adding the field @+2d.",
      "callout": {
        "title": "Two-thirds of rework comes from three causes",
        "body": "Missing photos (58), policy number mismatch (41) and wrong claim type (33): 132 of the 200 coded claims. All three can be checked on the lodgement form before triage sees the claim.",
        "accent": "#0ea5e9",
        "icon": "🔍"
      },
