      "page": {
        "title": "Findings — why claims are reworked",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "h3": "Why claims are reworked"
          },
          {
            "p": "200 reworked claims drawn at random from the 12 weeks, each coded by the first reason triage sent it back. Coded by me, checked independently by Tavita in triage."
          },
          {
            "ol": [
              "Missing photos or receipts — 58 (29%)",
              "Policy number doesn't match the customer — 41 (20.5%)",
              "Wrong claim type chosen — 33 (16.5%)",
              "Incident date outside the cover period — 22 (11%)",
              "Duplicate lodgement — 19 (9.5%)",
              "Bank details missing — 15 (7.5%)",
              "Other — 12 (6%)"
            ]
          },
          {
            "h4": "What this tells us"
          },
          {
            "p": "The top three causes are 66% of rework, and all three can be checked at lodgement. Incident date and duplicates (20.5% together) are rule checks the system could make. Bank details are disputed — see the requirements desk."
          },
          {
            "h4": "Caveats"
          },
          {
            "ul": [
              "First reason only — a claim can have more than one problem",
              "Excludes claims withdrawn before triage",
              "Storm weeks are represented roughly in proportion to their volume"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Triage team interviews — 5 people",
          "body": "What the triage team told me, paraphrased:\n\n- A large part of each day goes on chasing photos that could have come with the claim\n- Customers often type the old policy number from last year's renewal letter\n- There are 14 claim types; storm damage and water damage get mixed up constantly\n- They keep a side list of repeat callers that isn't in any system\n- Nobody measures time spent on rework, only the volume of it"
        }
